🔓 100% open source · self-hosted on your own Raspberry Pi
Your photos, your rules.Off the cloud, for good.
Off The Cloud is a NAS and a social network in one small box you own outright: your photos, videos, and documents back up in full resolution, get AI-tagged for instant search, and stay behind RAID-1 protected disks — with an optional, ethical social feed for sharing memories with the people who actually matter, free of ads, algorithms, and doomscrolling.
Get your device running
Raspberry Pi 5 image
Install script
For a Raspberry Pi 5 with at least 8 GB of RAM, a MicroSD card for the system and, optionally, two USB disks for RAID-1, plugged into the Pi's two blue USB ports. Use the official Raspberry Pi 27 W USB-C power supply (or a better one): with a weaker one the Pi can restart from low voltage while it processes your photos. No keyboard, monitor or Linux knowledge needed. Only have a 4 GB Pi? It can run, but only with Image Tagging and Face Recognition turned off in Settings, before you back up your photos: those models need more memory than 4 GB leaves.
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/image/off-the-cloud-rpi-lite-arm64.img.xz
⬇ Download the image (≈ 530 MB)
@a.href=https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/
Flash it to the MicroSD card with Raspberry Pi Imager: "Use custom", pick the file, write. Skip Imager's own customisation step.
Plug in the disks (into the two blue USB ports), insert the card and power the Pi on. After a minute it announces itself over Bluetooth.
Set it up from your phone with the app (iOS or Android): on the welcome screen tap Set up a new device. It finds the Pi over Bluetooth and runs the setup right there - your phone stays on its own WiFi. It asks for your WiFi, a name and password for the device and how to use the disks, then installs the software - about 20 minutes.
Sign in when it's done: the app connects to the new device by itself with the password you chose, or open https://<name>.off-the.cloud in a browser.
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/image/off-the-cloud-rpi-lite-arm64.img.xz.sha256
The image only carries the setup wizard: it installs the current release, so it never goes out of date. Checksum: .sha256.
One command on any machine you can already log into: Ubuntu, Debian, Raspberry Pi OS and other Debian-based distros (64-bit).
Terminal
Copy
# log into the machine, then: curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alonsovidales/otc/main/scripts/verified-install.sh \ | sudo bash -s -- <subdomain>
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc#readme
It installs everything and builds the software from source, then starts the service at http://<device-ip>:8080/. Safe to re-run to update. Your files go on two disks mirrored as RAID-1: it reuses a mirror it finds and only erases two new disks to build one when you confirm it, and it installs the status lights too. A single disk works as well (see the README). The WiFi setup isn't part of this path — see the image for a from-scratch Raspberry Pi.
@a.href=/account?signup=1
Want to reach it from outside your home network? Create an account for the bridge →
Get started
What happens under the hood
Both paths end in the same place: the image just runs the install script for you, after asking for your WiFi, a name and your disks from a phone.
1
Run the script
It installs MariaDB, Go, ONNX Runtime and the tagging model, then builds and starts the otc service, reachable straight away at http://<device-ip>:8080/ on your local network — safe to re-run any time to pick up updates.
2
Pick a subdomain
@a.href=/account
This names the device, e.g. pit for pit.off-the.cloud — the address your apps and friends use to reach it from outside your home network, instead of the local :8080 address. Register the name on your account page first, so it belongs to your bridge account (the image's setup does this for you).
3
Pair an app
Open the web, iOS, Android, macOS, Windows or Linux client, point it at your subdomain, and start backing up.
@a.href=#install
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc#readme
Starting from scratch on a Raspberry Pi 5? Use the Raspberry Pi 5 image: it wipes and mirrors two USB disks during setup, and if a Pi ever dies you flash a new card, attach the same disks and the wizard offers to recover everything. The status LEDs and every configuration option are in the README.
Computer apps
Keep your folders in sync
Back up folders from your computer, or keep them the same on both sides. The apps sit in the menu bar or tray and update themselves: when a new version is out, one click installs it.
Your system
Windows
Windows 10 or 11. Put the file somewhere permanent (not Downloads) and run it: it appears in the tray and starts at login.
@a.download
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/desktop/otc-sync-windows-amd64.exe
@a.download
